## 1. [Fundamental Equations of the Mathematical Model]
The state space within the body is defined by the following three dynamic variables, where each variable is a function of time $t$:

* $L(t)$ : The total number of leukemia stem cells (LSCs; the oncogenic drivers) harboring and hibernating within the bone marrow niche.
* $M(t)$ : The total number of leukemia cells awakened from hibernation and drawn out into the peripheral blood (and external environments) by existing agents such as G-CSF [5.1].
* $E(t)$ : The total number of genome-edited stealth immune cells (therapeutic cells) introduced exogenously via infusion [5.1].

The dynamics of each cell population at time $t$ follow the system of differential equations below:
$$\frac{dL}{dt} = \color{red}{- \lambda \cdot P(t) \cdot L} + r_L \cdot L \left(1 - \frac{L}{K_L}\right)$$ 
$$\frac{dM}{dt} = \color{red}{\lambda \cdot P(t) \cdot L} + r_M \cdot M \left(1 - \frac{M}{K_M}\right) - \color{blue}{\kappa \cdot E \cdot M}$$ 
$$\frac{dE}{dt} = - \mu_E \cdot E + \color{green}{\theta \cdot \frac{M}{M + \gamma} \cdot E} - \color{orange}{\sigma(t) \cdot E}$$ 
